---
import Project from "../../layouts/Project.astro";
---

<Project
  title="Grad Events & Newsletter Copilot"
  kind="ai workflow tool · solo, shipped"
  description="A production AI tool for Brown grad newsletter prep — campus events, relevance scoring, and on-voice drafts."
  lead="A production AI tool I built and deployed for my graduate-community work at Brown: it aggregates campus events, scores what relevant to grad students, and drafts the monthly newsletter — while users keep the editorial call. It cut users' prep from ~3 hours to ~15 minutes."
  links={[
    { label: "Open the live tool →", href: "https://sparks-livid.vercel.app/", primary: true },
    // { label: "Source code →", href: "https://github.com/keeeeeliu/sparks/tree/main/project-brown-grad-events-copilot" },
  ]}
  media={{
    kind: "video",
    src: "/project_gifs/grad-events-copilot.mp4",
    poster: "/project_gifs/grad-events-copilot-poster.jpg",
    alt: "Screen recording of the live Grad Events tool interface",
    caption: "End-to-end: set the date window → fetch & score events → pick what belongs → generate on-voice blurbs → copy into the newsletter.",
  }}
  facts={[
    { label: "Role", value: "Solo — build & ship" },
    { label: "Year", value: "2026" },
    { label: "For", value: "Brown grad community" },
    { label: "Impact", value: "~3h → ~15 min / issue" },
    { label: "Status", value: "Deployed & live" },
  ]}
  stack={["Next.js", "TypeScript", "Tailwind", "Zustand", "FastAPI", "Python", "Pydantic", "OpenAI", "Vercel", "Railway"]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Why I built it</h2>
    <p>
      Producing the monthly grad newsletter meant scanning scattered calendars, weeding out duplicate
      listings, judging what was relevant to grad students, and reformatting everything by hand — repetitive 
      work every cycle. So I built a copilot that does the gathering, de-duplication,
      and relevance scoring and drafts the newsletter, while the editorial judgment stays with me. It's
      part of my actual workflow now.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pulls structured events from Brown's LiveWhale calendar feed</li>
      <li>De-duplicates recurring / multi-source listings (e.g. 2026 June: 331 raw → 105 unique)</li>
      <li>
        LLM enrichment scores each event's grad-relevance, audience, and category — grounded, with a
        one-line rationale for every score
      </li>
      <li>Ranks and groups events into newsletter sections</li>
      <li>Generates on-voice blurbs (few-shot tuned to past newsletters) with one-click "improve"</li>
      <li>Human-in-the-loop throughout — users decide what makes the cut</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Engineering</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Multi-stage pipeline (ingest → dedupe → LLM enrichment → rank)</li>
      <li>Structured output validated with Pydantic, plus grounded / anti-hallucination extraction</li>
      <li>Cost &amp; latency telemetry — tokens and $ per run tracked in-app</li>
      <li>Evaluation harness measuring enrichment quality vs. hand labels </li>
      <li>Cut a full month's enrichment from ~207s → ~27s (~7.5×) with concurrent per-event calls</li>
      <li>Deployed &amp; live: Next.js on Vercel, FastAPI on Railway </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What's next (Wave B)</h2>
    <p>
      An agentic outreach assistant: a LangGraph agent drafts personalized emails to campus orgs —
      grounded in the events we already know about — then pauses for user's approval before anything sends.
      Human-in-the-loop, with persistent workflow state. (In progress.)
    </p>
  </section>
</Project>
